Rudra
All reports are TP. The Send and Sync implementations are genuinely unsound, and the crate exposes several independent safe-code paths to undefined behavior.
This content is only supported in a Feishu Docs
1. The Send implementations are unsound
All three containers implement Send without requiring their contained types to be Send:
unsafe impl<V> Send for SyncVec<V> {}

unsafe impl<K: Eq + Hash, V> Send for SyncBtreeMap<K, V> {}

unsafe impl<K: Eq + Hash, V> Send for SyncHashMap<K, V> {}
The containers physically own Vec<V>, BTreeMap<K, V>, or HashMap<K, V> inside an UnsafeCell.
Therefore, moving the container to another thread also moves its K and V values. At minimum, the bounds would need to be:
unsafe impl<V: Send> Send for SyncVec<V> {}

unsafe impl<K: Eq + Hash + Send, V: Send> Send
    for SyncHashMap<K, V>
{}

unsafe impl<K: Eq + Hash + Send, V: Send> Send
    for SyncBtreeMap<K, V>
{}
A mutex does not make a non-Send value safe to move between threads.
For example, the current implementation permits this safe-code pattern:
use std::cell::Cell;
use std::rc::Rc;
use std::thread;

let value = Rc::new(Cell::new(0));
let other = value.clone();

let container = SyncVec::from(vec![value]);

let handle = thread::spawn(move || {
    container.get(0).unwrap().set(1);
});

other.set(2);
handle.join().unwrap();
Rc<Cell<_>> is not Send. Because SyncVec<Rc<Cell<_>>> falsely implements Send, both threads can access the same non-atomic Cell and Rc state, creating a data race.
The maps have the same problem with, for example:
SyncHashMap<u32, Rc<Cell<u32>>>
SyncBtreeMap<u32, Rc<Cell<u32>>>
So Rudra’s PhantomSendForSend, NaiveSendForSend, and RelaxSend findings are correct.

---
2. The Sync implementations are even more clearly unsound
The crate claims all instantiations are Sync:
unsafe impl<V> Sync for SyncVec<V> {}
unsafe impl<K: Eq + Hash, V> Sync for SyncHashMap<K, V> {}
unsafe impl<K: Eq + Hash, V> Sync for SyncBtreeMap<K, V> {}
But the APIs return unguarded references directly into mutable collections.
SyncVec
These methods access the vector without retaining a lock:
pub fn get(&self, index: usize) -> Option<&V>
pub fn iter(&self) -> slice::Iter<'_, V>
pub fn dirty_ref(&self) -> &Vec<V>
pub fn len(&self) -> usize
pub fn is_empty(&self) -> bool
Meanwhile methods such as push, pop, clear, and shrink_to_fit mutate the same vector through &self.
This gives deterministic safe-code use-after-free even in one thread:
let vec = SyncVec::new();
vec.push(Box::new(42));

let reference = vec.get(0).unwrap();
vec.clear();                     // drops the Box
println!("{}", **reference);     // dangling reference
The borrow checker permits this because both get and clear take &self. The unsafe interior mutation invalidates the reference previously returned by the safe API.
Additionally, SyncVec::remove does not lock at all:
pub fn remove(&self, index: usize) -> Option<V> {
    let m = unsafe { &mut *self.dirty.get() };
    ...
}

SyncHashMap
get, iter, Index, dirty_ref, len, and is_empty read the hash map without holding a guard, while insert, remove, clear, and shrink_to_fit mutate it.
A deterministic safe-code failure is:
let map = SyncHashMap::new();
map.insert(1, Box::new(42));

let reference = map.get(&1).unwrap();
map.remove(&1);                  // removes and drops the Box
println!("{}", **reference);     // dangling reference
Concurrent insertion is worse: HashMap may reallocate its entire table while another thread holds a naked reference or iterator.
SyncBtreeMap
The B-tree implementation has the same defect:
pub fn get(...) -> Option<&V>
pub fn iter(&self) -> MapIter<'_, K, V>
pub fn dirty_ref(&self) -> &BTreeMap<K, V>
These references escape without any lock guard, while insert, remove, and clear mutate the tree through &self.
Removing an entry or rebalancing the tree can immediately invalidate returned references and iterators.

---
3. Adding Send/Sync bounds would not fix the Sync reports
For Send, adding the appropriate bounds fixes that particular auto-trait violation.
For Sync, bounds alone are insufficient. Even this would remain unsound:
unsafe impl<V: Send + Sync> Sync for SyncVec<V> {}
because the container can still mutate or drop values while naked &V references remain alive.
Similarly:
unsafe impl<K: Send + Sync, V: Send + Sync> Sync
    for SyncHashMap<K, V>
{}
does not prevent a concurrent hash-table reallocation from invalidating an iterator or reference.
The problem is not merely whether K and V are thread-safe. It is that the container violates Rust’s aliasing and lifetime rules.

---
4. get_mut is also incorrectly synchronized
The implementations form a mutable reference before acquiring the lock.
For example, SyncVec does:
let m = unsafe { &mut *self.dirty.get() };
let _g = self.lock.lock();
Some(VecRefMut {
    _g,
    value: Some(m.get_mut(index)?),
})

The hash map does the same thing.
Two threads can therefore simultaneously execute:
&mut *self.dirty.get()
before either has synchronized, creating overlapping mutable references. Locking afterward does not repair the aliasing violation that has already occurred.
The order must instead be conceptually:
let guard = self.lock.lock();
let collection = unsafe { &mut *self.dirty.get() };
But even that is only safe when every access follows the same locking discipline and references cannot outlive the guard.

---
5. The reentrant mutex creates another aliasing bug
SyncBtreeMap always uses ReentrantMutex<()>, and the other types may use it through a feature.
A reentrant lock allows the same thread to acquire the lock repeatedly. Consequently, safe code can potentially retain one SyncMapRefMut and call get_mut again for the same value:
let mut first = map.get_mut(&key).unwrap();
let mut second = map.get_mut(&key).unwrap();
Both contain an &mut V to the same object.
This is exactly why a normal reentrant mutex generally cannot safely expose mutable access to protected data. parking_lot::ReentrantMutexGuard itself deliberately provides shared rather than mutable access; this crate circumvents that restriction with a separate UnsafeCell.

